(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-arterial-occlusive-aneurysm-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arterial Occlusive Disease and Aortic Aneurysm Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"At what age does aortic dissection usually occur outside Marfan syndrome?","choices":["40s to 50s","Over 90","60s to 70s","20s to 30s"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection usually occurs in the 60s and 70s, except in Marfan syndrome, with males more affected than females and hypertension present in most cases. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which factor is associated with a decreased risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Family history","Male sex","Diabetes","Hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Decreased risk is associated with female sex, non-Caucasian race and diabetes; the reason for diabetes is unknown, possibly its treatment regimens or diabetic arterial stiffness. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"By what ratio do asymptomatic patients with significant carotid stenosis outnumber symptomatic ones?","choices":["10:1","4:1","2:1","1:1"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients with significant carotid stenosis outnumber symptomatic patients by 4:1, which is why carotid disease is often found before any stroke or attack. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Carotid angioplasty with stenting is chosen for which patients?","choices":["Poor surgical candidates","Those with stenosis under 50%","Every asymptomatic patient","Those under 5 years' survival"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carotid stenting, most commonly through the common femoral artery, is less invasive and allows intervention in poor surgical candidates; endarterectomy remains the gold standard. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of a marginally threatened limb?","choices":["Salvageable only with immediate revascularization","Major tissue loss is inevitable","Under no threat of tissue loss","Salvageable if treated promptly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A marginally threatened limb is salvageable if promptly treated, with minimal toe pain and no sensory loss or muscle weakness; an immediately threatened limb needs immediate revascularization. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of untreated acute aortic dissection?","choices":["Regression under 2 cm","High mortality","Spontaneous full recovery","Slow, harmless enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute untreated aortic dissection carries a high mortality rate, which is why pressure is lowered at once and diseased aorta is grafted and replaced surgically. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What is the minimum exercise prescription for peripheral artery disease?","choices":["60 minutes, twice weekly","15 minutes, five times weekly","30-45 minutes, three times weekly","10 minutes, once weekly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The minimum is 30 to 45 minutes three times per week of consistent moderate exercise, which improves walking distance, ideally within a supervised rehabilitation program. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute limb ischemia?","choices":["Loss of the ankle reflex","Weakness of toe extension","Numbness over the dorsum of the foot","Paralysis of the calf muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so sensory deficits over the dorsum of the foot are often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is amaurosis fugax?","choices":["Transient loss of vision in one eye","Permanent loss of vision in both eyes","Double vision on looking sideways","Flashing lights before a headache"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amaurosis fugax is interrupted blood flow to the retina causing transient monocular blindness, a rapid fading of vision like a curtain descending, a focal symptom of carotid disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which feature separates peripheral neuropathy from claudication?","choices":["Pain relieved by rest","Pain in the calf muscle","Pain brought on by walking","Pain unaffected by exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral neuropathy, common in older patients with diabetes or alcoholism, is a continuous burning sensation in the foot that exercise does not affect, unlike claudication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which study is best for abdominal aortic aneurysm diagnosis and pre-intervention planning?","choices":["Abdominal ultrasound","Abdominal X-ray","Transesophageal echocardiography","Computed tomography angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography angiography is the best diagnostic and pre-intervention planning study, accurately delineating the location, size and extent of the aneurysm. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is peripheral bypass grafting principally used to treat?","choices":["Severe critical limb ischemia","Mild intermittent claudication","Asymptomatic carotid narrowing","Small abdominal aneurysms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bypass grafting is invasive, highly effective and principally used for severe critical leg ischemia, bypassing diseased segments with a saphenous vein or synthetic graft. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which history question best measures claudication?","choices":["Did the pain follow a cold?","How far can you walk before pain?","Does coughing make the pain worse?","Is the pain worse after meals?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The history asks where the pain is, how far the patient can walk before it starts, whether that distance has changed, and how long rest takes to relieve it. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which set is the six P's of arterial assessment?","choices":["Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, purpura","Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia","Pain, pallor, pulselessness, pruritus, paralysis, poikilothermia","Pain, pallor, petechiae, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The six P's are pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia (difficulty regulating body temperature), with a cold, cyanotic limb and absent pulses distal to the occlusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is urgent vascular referral needed regardless of aneurysm size?","choices":["Early satiety alone","A new diagnosis of diabetes","A single high blood pressure","Aneurysmal pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A patient who complains of aneurysmal pain needs urgent vascular referral regardless of aneurysm size, because pain occurs with expansion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"How does ischemic rest pain respond to analgesics?","choices":["Poorly, even to narcotics","Completely, to simple analgesics","Only to anti-inflammatory drugs","Well, once the leg is raised"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain is not readily controlled by analgesics, even narcotics; it is relieved instead by dependency, hanging the leg over the bed or walking around. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does an irreversibly ischemic limb require?","choices":["Urgent bypass grafting","Heparin and observation","Major amputation","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An irreversibly ischemic limb has major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage, profound sensory loss and paralysis, and needs major amputation whatever therapy is instituted. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What does the word part \"athero\" in atherosclerosis refer to?","choices":["The clot at a site of rupture","The fatty core of the plaque","The fibrous cap over the plaque","The calcium in the arterial media"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Athero means gruel or paste and refers to the fatty material in the core of the plaque; sclerosis (hardness) refers to the fibrous tissue that stiffens the wall over it. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which acute limb ischemia categories leave time for vascular imaging?","choices":["Immediately threatened only","Irreversibly damaged only","All four categories","Viable and marginally threatened"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viable and marginally threatened limbs have sufficient time for vascular imaging such as computed tomography angiography; immediately threatened limbs go to a surgical suite. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What causes the pain of a pseudoaneurysm?","choices":["Swelling or nerve compression","Muscle ischemia on walking","A tear through the intima","Retroperitoneal bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain from increased pressure due to swelling, or from nerve compression, at the site of the arterial leak. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Where do diminished or absent pulses appear in peripheral artery disease?","choices":["Below the stenosis","Above the stenosis","Only in the arms","Only at the stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulses are diminished or absent below the level of the arterial stenosis or occlusion, which is why pulses are checked from the brachial and radial down to the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is true of aneurysms in women despite their lower incidence?","choices":["They rupture more often","They never need repair","They grow only in the chest","They rupture less often"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Female sex is associated with a decreased risk of aneurysm, although women with aneurysms rupture more often, which is why their repair threshold is lower at 5.0 cm. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Why does walking around relieve ischemic rest pain, unlike claudication?","choices":["Movement empties the leg veins","Dependency lets gravity aid flow","Muscle work builds new vessels","Walking lowers blood pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In contrast to claudication, walking around uses the gravity effect of dependence on limb perfusion, just as hanging the leg over the edge of the bed does. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which growth rate indicates repair of a thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Over 0.1 cm per year","Over 0.5 cm per year","Over 0.5 cm per month","Over 2 cm per year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A growth rate over 0.5 cm per year indicates surgical repair of a thoracic aneurysm; for the abdominal aorta, rapid growth is 0.5 cm in six months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which history is common in thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Recent viral illness","Hypothyroidism","Hypertension","Diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A history of hypertension is common in thoracic aortic aneurysm, and management includes aggressive blood pressure control, beta blockers and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"How often is a thoracic aortic aneurysm under 4.5 cm imaged?","choices":["Every 6-12 months","Every month","Every 10 years","Every 3-5 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A root, ascending or descending aneurysm under 4.5 cm is imaged with computed tomography or magnetic resonance every 3 to 5 years; 4.5 to 5.4 cm every 6 to 12 months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What makes up atheroemboli?","choices":["Clot formed in the left atrium","Air drawn into a vein","Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Fat droplets from bone marrow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How is an ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm usually repaired?","choices":["Open repair with root replacement","Thrombin injection into the sac","Endovascular repair only","Medical therapy with no repair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An ascending thoracic aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement, while a descending one can be repaired open or endovascularly. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which vasculitis is listed among the causes of aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Medial calcific sclerosis","Leriche syndrome","Arteriolar sclerosis","Takayasu arteritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The listed vasculitides are Takayasu arteritis and giant cell arteritis, alongside Marfan syndrome, syphilis, trauma and congenital aortic aneurysms. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What does an endarterectomy do?","choices":["Dissolves clot through a catheter","Removes plaque through open surgery","Dilates the artery with a balloon","Reroutes flow around the blockage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endarterectomy is invasive surgery that removes plaque directly from inside the artery through an open incision; amputation is the last surgical option. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"}]};
